WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:07.540
After Watson and Crick came up with their hypothesis

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.540
regarding the structure of DNA,

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.880
the scientific world was now curious to know

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.880
how it could be replicated ?

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.780
They put forth a hypothesis that the DNA

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.360
replicates in a semiconservative manner.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.740
That is, both strands separate and each serves

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.040
as a template for the synthesis of new strands.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.020
Template in literal terms

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.640
means a pattern used as a sample to make something.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.580
However, the scientific world was not ready to accept this fact easily.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:43.140
So there were three theories put forth which hinted

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.460
at the replication process of the DNA double helix.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.360
One was called the conservative model,

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.360
the other was the Dispersive model

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.380
and the last was the semiconservative model.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.660
Let's first understand what each method had to say.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.780
The first model in the list is the conservative model.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.960
This model suggested that two DNA strands separate

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.640
and serve as templates for replication.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.200
But soon after replication, the strands joined back

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.200
to form the original parent DNA,

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:22.520
while the new synthesised strands joined to form a completely new DNA.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.980
Thus, we get a newly synthesised DNA molecule.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:31.340
And we also get the original parent DNA molecule back, which is conserved.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.380
Hence the name conservative replication.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:41.180
Now comes the next one, this was called the Dispersive model of replication.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.440
As the name suggests,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.320
Dispersive means to disperse or scatter.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.200
So what gets dispersed?

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.420
Well, according to this model the DNA which is about to replicate

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.920
breaks into small fragments.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.140
The new strands get synthesised in between.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:04.380
As a result, we get a hybrid of both new and old strands together.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.520
The old parental strands appear to be dispersed

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.900
in the newly synthesised one.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.860
Hence, this method is named as Dispersive.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.420
Now comes the last model

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:20.280
It was called the semiconservative model of replication.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:24.540
The model suggested that two strands of DNA unwind

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.540
and serve as templates for newer strands.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:33.560
As a result, the replicated DNA's contained one old and one new strand.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.540
Hence the name semiconservative.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.240
Nevertheless, this model was not accepted initially

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.260
when put forth by Watson and Crick.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.520
However, one of the most amazing experiments in biology

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.820
named the Meselson and Stahl experiment helped crack this mystery.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.300
Two geneticists Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.980
carried out an experiment around 1958.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.100
They used a heavy isotope of nitrogen and inserted it

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:08.900
in the DNA of the bacterium Escherichia Coli.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.400
They obtained several generations of E.coli and

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.580
analysed each for the presence of DNA.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.440
The results helped them understand

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:24.420
that the replication of DNA in E.coli is indeed semiconservative.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:30.080
This is how DNA replication takes place in almost all organisms.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:34.060
Now, let me introduce you to one more interesting term.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.720
It's called the Central Dogma.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.000
Ever heard of this?

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.520
Well it's an important term,

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.220
and an equally interesting concept in biology.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.600
The "Central Dogma in Molecular biology"

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.280
is used to explain the flow of genetic information.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.340
And what does that mean?

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.080
We all know that DNA governs life.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:00.740
But any idea how this mechanism works?

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:05.400
Let's understand this with the help of the Central dogma.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:12.640
In a cell the DNA from the nucleus replicates to make a copy of itself.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.660
This is what we're aware of

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:19.800
And do we know what the functional segment of DNA is?

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.700
It's a gene. It's the segment of DNA that codes for a particular character

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.680
The part of DNA which acts as a Gene

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.060
gets copied in the form of a single strand.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.980
This is called mRNA.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:41.060
This mRNA that is the messenger RNA to be precise

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.660
is a single strand copy of the DNA.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:48.120
It comes out of the nucleus in the cytoplasm and then,

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.360
is worked upon by the special machinery of enzymes.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:56.920
The enzyme machinery reads the code hidden in the mRNA

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.620
and synthesises proteins accordingly.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:05.020
This complete process of mRNA synthesis from the DNA

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.920
followed by the synthesis of proteins from the

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.580
mRNA is called as Central dogma.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:16.240
In other words this simplest process by which proteins

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.960
are synthesised from the code of the DNA molecule

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:21.740
is the Central Dogma of life.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.180
This is how it's usually summarised.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:29.620
Did you know that these two steps have a scientific name?

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.160
The process in which the mRNA is synthesised

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.780
from the DNA strand is called transcription.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.180
And the process in which proteins are synthesised

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:44.560
from the code of mRNA is called translation.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:48.140
Would you want to know more about these interesting processes?

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:51.220
For that, tune into our upcoming videos.

